'''Oocystaceae''' is a [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] of [[green algae]], in the class [[Trebouxiophyceae]].<ref name=AB_t4962>{{AlgaeBase taxon|name=Oocystaceae|id=4962|access-date=2022-03-02}}</ref> [[Molecular phylogenetic]] studies mostly (but not always) place the family in the order [[Chlorellales]], as sister to [[Chlorellaceae]].<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.3389/fevo.2014.00063|doi-access=free |title=New phylogenetic hypotheses for the core Chlorophyta based on chloroplast sequence data |date=2014 |last1=Fučíková |first1=Karolina |last2=Leliaert |first2=Frederik |last3=Cooper |first3=Endymion D. |last4=Škaloud |first4=Pavel |last5=d'Hondt |first5=Sofie |last6=De Clerck |first6=Olivier |last7=Gurgel |first7=Carlos F. D. |last8=Lewis |first8=Louise A. |last9=Lewis |first9=Paul O. |last10=Lopez-Bautista |first10=Juan M. |last11=Delwiche |first11=Charles F. |last12=Verbruggen |first12=Heroen |journal=Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution |volume=2 |page=63 |bibcode=2014FrEEv...2...63F |hdl=11343/118338 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> The [[Type (biology)|type]] genus is ''[[Oocystis]]''.

Members of Oocystaceae are common microalgae in freshwater habitats, and a few occur in semi-saline.<ref name=Liu2018/>

==Description==
Members of the family Oocystaceae consist of cells which are solitary or found in colonies of 2, 4, 8, 16, or more. Cells are typically oval to ellipsoidal, sometimes spherical, rhomboid, spindle-shaped or lemon-shaped. Cells, when colonial, may be arranged into [[coenobium (morphology)|coenobia]] or pseudofilaments. Cells contain multiple [[chloroplast]]s of varying morphology—usually parietal, sometimes radial or spongiform, and with or without a [[pyrenoid]]. The [[cell wall]] is multilayered, often thickened at the poles, and may be smooth, warty or covered in spines.<ref name=Stenclova>{{cite thesis|title=Systematic revision of the family Oocystaceae|last1=Štenclová|first1=Lenka|date=2013|pages=70|location=České Budějovice|publisher=University of South Bohemia}}</ref>

A uniting [[ultrastructural]] feature of all members of this family is the multiple-layered cell wall, with the [[cellulose]] fibrils in each layer organized perpendicularly to those of adjoining layers.<ref name=Stenclova/>

Reproduction occurs [[asexual reproduction|asexually]] and via the production of multiple [[autospore]]s. The daughter cells often remain in the expanded mother cell wall.<ref name=Stenclova/>

==Classification==
Traditionally, genera and species within Oocystaceae have been separated from each other based on morphology. However, microalgae typically have very few morphological features for classification, and the morphological features may have [[convergent evolution|evolved more than once]]. Currently, [[molecular phylogenetic]]s form the basis of species and genera.<ref name=Stenclova/>

Morphologically, there were three [[subfamilies]]: Eremosphaeroideae, Lagerheimioideae and Oocystoideae. Molecular data has revised this to the three current subfamilies: [[Eremosphaeroideae]], [[Makinoelloideae]], and [[Oocystoideae]].<ref name=Stenclova/>

